<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  This app can open a socket, and this comment used to say it never would. What
  changed and what did not is the whole of the note.

  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" /> is below because
  a Sammlung can now arrive from the editor over the home network instead of on a
  memory stick. SPEC.md 5.2's sanctioned case is a METACOM licensee preparing
  material "for the person they support" and putting it "on that person's
  device" — which is the direction that permission is here to serve, and the only
  direction PackageReceiver runs.

  WHAT THIS COST. The old argument was structural and it was a good one: a viewer
  with no INTERNET permission cannot move a non-redistributable package's bytes
  off the device, because it cannot move bytes anywhere. That guarantee is gone
  and cannot be got back by wording. Anyone reading this to find out whether
  SPEC.md 5.2 is still enforceable here deserves the narrower claim rather than
  the old one restated.

  WHAT IS TRUE INSTEAD. The app receives a package and has no code path that
  sends one. Concretely, and each part is checkable:

    - PackageReceiver is a server and there is no client. Nothing in this app
      opens an outbound connection, builds a URL, or reads a response; the
      permission buys a listening socket and is spent entirely on that. Note
      that android:usesCleartextTraffic is deliberately *not* set: it governs
      the platform's HTTP client stacks, a raw ServerSocket is unaffected by it,
      and setting it would be claiming a client this app does not have.
    - That socket has exactly one route. POST /paket, plus the OPTIONS a browser
      sends before it. No GET, no listing, no path that returns a package's bytes
      or its metadata — a socket that could serve them is precisely the path
      5.2 forbids.
    - The socket is open only while the receive screen is on screen. No
      foreground service; bound on entering, closed on leaving and on
      backgrounding, so nothing is listening while a child is using the board.

  WHAT KEEPS IT TRUE. PackageReceiverTest sweeps every method against the route
  and every plausible path against the router, and fails if anything but the one
  POST is served. The guarantee moved from "structurally impossible" to "one
  route, POST only, enforced by a test", and that test is the load-bearing part
  of this comment. Adding a second route means editing it, which is the point.

  The other half of the old note still stands and is untouched by any of this:
  SPEC.md 1 and 5 make "resolves no reference" a property of the format, every
  image and every sound is baked into the package, and nothing here fetches
  anything at render time — because nothing here fetches anything at all.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:localeConfig="@xml/locales_config"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Vorlaut"
        tools:targetApi="33">

        <!--
          Turning the tablet must not restart the app. Without this Android
          destroys and recreates the activity on every rotation, and the board
          comes back on its start page with the sentence bar empty: `route` and
          `opened` in MainActivity are composition state, so a recreation sends
          the front-door effect round again and reopens the package from the
          top. A child mid-sentence whose tablet gets picked up loses the
          sentence.

          Handling the change ourselves is honest rather than a dodge, because
          there is nothing left for Android to do about it: AlwaysLandscape
          draws the board landscape whatever shape the window is, and no
          resource here is qualified by orientation. Compose simply remeasures.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|smallestScreenSize"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              Opening a .obz from a file manager, a download, or a messaging app.

              Both the media type SPEC.md 2 gives the format and the one providers
              actually report for an unregistered extension are matched, plus a
              path pattern for the cases where a provider offers no type at all.
              An over-broad filter is the right trade here: a file that turns out
              not to be a board package is refused by the importer with a reason
              the person can act on, whereas a filter too narrow to see the file
              leaves them with no way in at all.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/import_board_package">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="content" />
                <data android:scheme="file" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/zip" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/x-zip-compressed" />
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/import_board_package">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="content" />
                <data android:scheme="file" />
                <data android:host="*" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/octet-stream" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\.obz" />
                <data android:pathPattern=".*\\..*\\.obz" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- Sharing a package to the app from elsewhere. -->
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/import_board_package">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/zip" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/x-zip-compressed" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/octet-stream" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

    </application>

</manifest>
